  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe proposed strategy focuses on minimizing the maximum overlap in the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by employing a hybrid optimization approach. The central idea is to iteratively refine the step function h using a combination of gradient-based updates and targeted perturbations, focusing on the shifts where the overlap is largest. By leveraging Fast Fourier Transform (FFT)-based convolution for efficient overlap computation and maintaining feasibility through repair mechanisms, this approach aims to escape local minima and find a lower C\u2085 value. The key innovations include: (a) Efficient computation of overlap integrals using FFT convolution, (b) Targeted optimization focusing on the most significant overlaps, (c) Adaptive gradient updates with random perturbations for robust exploration.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Let h \u2208 \u211d^{n_points} represent the discretized function, with h_i \u2208 [0, 1] for all i.\n- Enforce the constraint \u2211h_i dx = 1, where dx = 2.0 / n_points.\n- Define the correlation at shift k as (h * (1 - h))[k], where * denotes convolution.\n- The objective is C\u2085 = max_k (h * (1 - h))[k] * dx.\n- We use FFT-based convolution to compute the overlaps efficiently, then focus optimization on the shifts with the highest values.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nA. Initialization\n1. Read initial_h_values if available; else initialize h randomly.\n2. Set n_points to 200 for higher resolution.\n3. Interpolate initial_h_values to match n_points if provided.\n4. Clip h to [0,1] and normalize to ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n\nB. Convolution and overlap computation\n1. Compute the convolution h * (1 - h) using FFT-based convolution.\n2. Multiply by dx to obtain the overlap values for each shift.\n3. Identify the shift(s) with the maximum overlap.\n\nC. Targeted gradient-based optimization\n1. Compute the gradient of the overlap at the maximum shift with respect to h.\n2. Update h using an adaptive learning rate, focusing on reducing the maximum overlap.\n3. Apply random perturbations to h with a probability based on stagnation.\n4. Clip h to [0,1] and normalize to maintain the integral constraint.\n\nD. Iterative refinement\n1. Repeat overlap computation, gradient updates, and repairs until budget exhausted or convergence.\n2. Periodically adjust learning rate based on progress and apply perturbations to escape local minima.\n3. Track the best h found that minimizes C\u2085.\n\nE. Validation and return\n1. Ensure final h satisfies 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n_points / 2.\n2. Compute C\u2085 using FFT-based convolution for the best h.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Default: n_points=200, max_iter=10000, learning_rate=0.001\n- Variations: n_points=150 (faster), n_points=250 (higher precision), perturb_rate=0.1\n- Adjust learning rate decay based on progress, e.g., halving every 1000 steps without improvement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each iteration: FFT convolution ~1ms, gradient computation ~5ms, update ~1ms\n- Total steps within 600s: ~10000 iterations\n- Robustness via random perturbations and periodic restarts on stagnation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Check h_i \u2208 [0,1] and \u2211h = n_points / 2\n- Validate C\u2085 using FFT-based convolution to ensure consistency\n- Return the best h, C\u2085, and n_points",
